        "        periodicity             : 2*pi (360 degrees), range covered in FES: [-180, +180)\n"
        "        definition              : IUPAC dihedral formula\n"
        "                                    phi = atan2( n1 x n2 ),\n"
        "                                   n1 = (b2) x (b1 - b2),\n"
        "                                   n2 = (b3 - b2) x (b3),\n"
        "                                   b1 = N - C_ACE,  b2 = CA - N,  b3 = C - CA.\n"
        "\n"
        "CV 2: psi  (psi backbone dihedral of residue CYS)\n"
        "        atom indices (0-based)  : (6, 8, 15, 17)\n"
        "        atom names              : (N of CYS, CA of CYS, C of CYS, N of NME)\n"
        "        PDB serials (1-based)   : (7, 9, 16, 18)\n"
        "        periodicity             : 2*pi (360 degrees), range covered in FES: [-180, +180)\n"
        "        definition              : IUPAC dihedral formula\n"
        "                                    psi = atan2( m1 x m2 ),\n"
        "                                   m1 = (b2) x (b1 - b2),\n"
        "                                   m2 = (b3 - b2) x (b3),\n"
        "                                   b1 = CA - N,  b2 = C - CA, b3 = N_NME - C.\n"
        "\n"
        "Why phi and psi (and not omega)?\n"
        "    The Ramachandran coordinates (phi, psi) of residue CYS are the\n"
        "    biologically meaningful backbone degrees of freedom that span all\n"
        "    canonical secondary-structure basins.  In the ACE-CYS-NME dipeptide\n"
        "    omega (the peptide bond dihedral, here C_ACE-N-CA-C of residue CYS at\n"
        "    indices (4, 6, 8, 15)) is constrained rigidly to the trans value\n"
        "    +-180 degrees by the AMBER ff14SB planarity/periodicity term\n"
        "    (k_omega ~ 10 kcal/mol with n=2 multiplet) and the lack of\n"
        "    donor/acceptor to break this barrier in vacuum.  Sampling omega\n"
        "    would not change because the energy gap between cis and trans is\n"
        "    about 10 kcal/mol and Metadynamics with hills of width 0.1 rad\n"
        "    on omega would never overcome it in any feasible run length.\n"
        "    Using omega as a CV would ruthlessly waste degrees of freedom\n"
        "    and miss the actual conformational free-energy landscape that\n"
        "    the Ramachandran plot (phi, psi) is designed to display.\n"
        "\n"
        "Why a Hill sigma = 0.10 rad (about 5.73 degrees)?\n"
        "    The smallest barrier between known basins (C7eq <-> alpha_R) is\n"
        "    about 5-7 kJ/mol at the phi=psi=0 ridge.  A sigma significantly\n"
        "    smaller under-smooths the FES and creates fictitious wells.  A\n"
        "    sigma significantly larger (~>=15 degrees) makes individual hills\n"
        "    indistinguishable from the natural basin widths and slows\n"
        "    convergence considerably.  0.10 rad is the standard choice for\n"
        "    alanine/CYS dipeptides in vacuum (Barducci, Bonomi, Parrinello,\n"
        "    WIREs Comput. Mol. Sci. 1, 826 (2011)).\n"
        "\n"
